OCWORLD 1
# Written by the Aver Engine editor. Centimetres, +X forward, +Y right, +Z up.
ID 0x122E9F7824A2AF12
NAME Range
BUILD 0
ALGO 3
SUN dir 0.304027 0.406402 0.861629 color 1 1 1 lux 100000   # elev 59.5 azim 53.2
SKY model physical mie 0.0028 multiscatter 1.9 steps 32 aerial 4
FOG exp density 2e-05 color 0.7 0.78 0.88

# Ground: four slabs tiled around a rectangular gap, not the single slab this used to be. A plain
# PLACE/PLACEG defaults collide=true (OcWorld.hpp), so each piece below is still a real static body --
# something to stand on, not a floating camera -- and it still matters for the reason it always did:
# the character fires a ray from its own eye, and a floor is what stops that ray leaving the world when
# you aim down. Only the shape changed, not that fact. A single PLACEG box has no way to have a hole
# cut into it, and the pool basin below needs one, so the gap is left by tiling four boxes around it
# instead of placing one across it -- two full-depth strips either side of the gap along X, and two
# shorter strips filling the gap's own column along Y. Every piece keeps the original slab's z -10,
# half-extent-z 10 and M_Floor material; only x, y and the horizontal half-extents differ per piece.
PLACEG Meshes/cube.ocmesh -1910 0 -10 0 0 0 2090 4000 10 M_Floor      # full depth, x < 180
PLACEG Meshes/cube.ocmesh 2410 0 -10 0 0 0 1590 4000 10 M_Floor       # full depth, x > 820
PLACEG Meshes/cube.ocmesh 500 2435 -10 0 0 0 320 1565 10 M_Floor      # gap column, y > 870
PLACEG Meshes/cube.ocmesh 500 -1785 -10 0 0 0 320 2215 10 M_Floor     # gap column, y < 430

# The pool: a basin sunk through that gap, floor and four walls built the same way the ground slab is
# -- ordinary collide=true PLACEG cubes, not a special primitive. The ground slab's top sits at z 0
# (centre -10, half-extent-z 10, so -10 + 10 = 0), and the basin's walls run from z -150 up to exactly
# that surface, so the rim sits flush with the yard instead of leaving a step or floating a seam above
# it. The floor sits at z -160 with half-extent-z 10, putting its own top at -150 -- meeting the walls'
# bottom exactly, no gap a player could drop through between them. The floor spans the walls' full
# footprint (320 x 220), not just the water's interior (300 x 200): a floor only as wide as the water
# would leave open ground under each wall's own thickness, and a player standing at the rim would fall
# through there instead of finding solid concrete.
#
# WHERE: centred at (500, 650) -- to the +Y side of the straight line from spawn to the dead-ahead
# target (the corridor along y 0 from x 0 to x 600), forward of the crates and short of the row of
# targets at x 600. The nearest wall face sits at y 430, clear of that corridor by 430 cm, and clear of
# the +Y target's own spawn-to-target line by at least 130 cm along its whole length -- that line only
# reaches y 300 even at its far end, x 600, where the target itself sits. Close enough to the spawn
# point to be seen without hunting for it; nowhere near the one thing this template needs to keep
# instant.
PLACEG Meshes/cube.ocmesh 500 650 -160 0 0 0 320 220 10 M_Concrete    # pit floor
PLACEG Meshes/cube.ocmesh 810 650 -75 0 0 0 10 220 75 M_Concrete      # +X wall
PLACEG Meshes/cube.ocmesh 190 650 -75 0 0 0 10 220 75 M_Concrete      # -X wall
PLACEG Meshes/cube.ocmesh 500 860 -75 0 0 0 300 10 75 M_Concrete      # +Y wall
PLACEG Meshes/cube.ocmesh 500 440 -75 0 0 0 300 10 75 M_Concrete      # -Y wall

# The water itself: the surface a WATER record authors instead of the old --water CLI flag (see
# OcWaterPlacement's own comment in modules/formats/include/aver/formats/OcWorld.hpp). Bounded, not
# infinite, because this is a pool and not an ocean -- `bounds` takes the interior the walls enclose
# (200..800, 450..850), matching the basin's own water footprint rather than the wider slab around it.
# Level -20 sits 20 cm below the rim at z 0, a real pool's coping edge rather than a surface brimming
# level with the deck.
#
# SIMULATED, not analytic. `simulate` makes this surface a soft body the physics solver sloshes --
# water that a player walking into it actually disturbs, and that can slop up and over the rim and
# stay there -- instead of a Gerstner sum evaluated in a vertex shader, which is beautiful on an
# ocean and inert in a swimming pool. A template exists to show what the engine can do, and a pool
# that reacts is the whole reason for putting one here.
#
# No WAVE record follows this one, and under `simulate` there could not be a meaningful one: waves
# describe an analytic surface, and this surface's shape comes from the solver. (Zero waves is also
# the format's own documented behaviour for flat water, so a level that drops `simulate` still gets
# a sensible still pool rather than the open swell four hardcoded waves used to give every body of
# water regardless of size.)
WATER name Pool level -20 bounds 200 450 800 850 simulate

# Two crates -- set dressing, and also honest raycast targets: ordinary collide=true placements, so
# shooting one produces a REAL hit whose entity is stamped by LevelInstance's own static-box path. It
# simply has no graph to react, so nothing scores. Worth seeing at least once: a hit on something that
# does not care looks different from a miss, and both are visible in the log.
PLACE  Meshes/cube.ocmesh 250 -150 60 0 0 0 60 M_Crate
PLACE  Meshes/cube.ocmesh -250 -150 60 0 0 0 60 M_Crate

# The game mode. A graph class is a class like any other -- placed, not registered through some
# separate mechanism. See AN_FPRules.ocgraph for why a headless log shows TWO of these ticking.
PLACE none 0 0 0 0 0 0 1 class AN_FPRules

# THE PLAYER IS NOT PLACED HERE, ON PURPOSE. AN_FPRules names it with `pawn=AN_FPCharacter`, and
# aver_fw_begin_play spawns it and POSSESSES it -- the same division a UE GameMode's DefaultPawnClass
# uses. Possession is what makes the camera follow it at all, so a placed-but-unpossessed character
# would render the whole level from a default camera; placing one HERE as well would simply add a
# second, unpossessed character standing next to the real one.

# THE TARGETS ARE PLACED HERE, LIKE ANY OTHER LEVEL CONTENT -- the single biggest difference from the
# earlier starter template, which had the character SPAWN its targets and remember their ids in VARs.
# That was never a design; it was a workaround for a missing capability. Now that `Raycast.entity`
# returns the owning scene entity, a target is just a thing in the level -- move these, duplicate them,
# delete one, and the character's graph neither knows nor cares.
#
# THEY ARE Character-PARENTED, WHICH LOOKS ODD AND IS DELIBERATE -- see AN_FPTarget.ocgraph's own
# "becoming shootable" comment. A `class=` placement gets NO physics body from anywhere in this engine
# (LevelInstance's static-box path is skipped for class placements, and ClassBuilder cannot add a
# collider), so a plain Actor target would be invisible to the very ray meant to hit it.
#
# LAID OUT ALONG +X, WHICH IS WHERE THE PLAYER IS ALREADY LOOKING. A character spawns at yaw 0, and
# AverCharacter.LookDirection at yaw 0 pitch 0 is exactly (1, 0, 0) -- so +X is dead ahead on the first
# frame, before the mouse has moved. The middle target is straight down that line on purpose: press
# Space the instant the level opens and you score, with no hunting for what this template does.
PLACE none 600 0 100 0 0 0 1 class AN_FPTarget
PLACE none 600 300 100 0 0 0 1 class AN_FPTarget
PLACE none 600 -300 100 0 0 0 1 class AN_FPTarget
